\title{Three Structural Barrier Theorems for Integer Factorization}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{August 12, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We prove three unconditional, independent obstructions that classify why broad classes
of \emph{structural} approaches to integer factorization cannot succeed. Let $N=pq$ be a
semiprime with $p\neq q$ prime.
\textbf{Barrier~I (algebraic):} for every $f\in\Z[x]$ and every prime $p\mid N$ we have
$p\mid f(N)\iff p\mid f(0)$, whence $\gcd(f(N),N)=\gcd(f(0),N)$; a fixed invariant
exposes only the primes dividing $f(0)$ --- at most $\log_2|f(0)|$ of them --- and if it
splits $N=pq$ then $\min(p,q)\le|f(0)|$. No finite family of integer polynomials, and no
family indexed by the residue class of $N$ modulo a fixed integer, splits every
semiprime; the barrier extends to rational invariants, where the denominator contributes
exactly one further input-independent source of primes.
\textbf{Barrier~II (symmetry):} a quantity $D(p,q)$ is a function of the product $N=pq$
\emph{if and only if} it is symmetric --- a sharp dichotomy, so antisymmetric data (the
gap $p-q$, ``the left factor'') is not a function of $N$, while $\min(p,q)$ and $p+q$
are.
\textbf{Barrier~III (holomorphic rigidity):} an entire function vanishing at two
distinct points $a\neq b$ equals $(z-a)(z-b)G(z)$ with $G$ entire, with $G$ nonvanishing
off $\{a,b\}$ when the zero set is exactly $\{a,b\}$; independently, the zero set of a
nonzero entire function is countable and Lebesgue-null, so zero-set search succeeds with
probability zero; and such devices do exist, so the barrier is rigidity, not
nonexistence.
We prove the bridges between the barriers, prove that they are pairwise non-implying,
and locate the exact escape route: Pollard's $p-1$ method splits semiprimes, but for
each \emph{fixed} exponent it is a constant polynomial and falls to Barrier~I. All of
its power comes from letting the exponent grow with the input, and this --- unbounded
description length --- is the only loophole the three barriers leave.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} integer factorization, polynomial invariants, gcd witnesses,
symmetric functions, entire functions, holomorphic rigidity, Pollard's $p-1$, RSA.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The shape of a structural factoring proposal}

Let $N=pq$ be an RSA-like modulus: a product of two distinct primes of roughly equal bit
length. A recurring genre of factoring proposal has the following shape.
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\arabic*)]
  \item From $N$ alone, construct a mathematical object $\mathcal{O}_N$ --- an integer, a
        polynomial, a lattice, a matrix, an analytic function.
  \item Extract a number from $\mathcal{O}_N$ --- a resultant, a discriminant, a
        determinant, a hyperdeterminant, a short lattice vector, a zero.
  \item Take a greatest common divisor with $N$, or read the factors off directly.
\end{enumerate}
Concrete instances are legion, and each fails in practice. This paper explains the
failures structurally: we identify three properties of the map $(p,q)\mapsto pq$ that
make entire classes of such proposals impossible, and prove that the three properties
are logically independent.

\subsection{Why unconditional barriers matter}

The results below assume nothing: no appeal to $\mathrm{P}\neq\mathrm{NP}$, to the
hardness of factoring, or to any unproven conjecture. They are congruence, symmetry and
rigidity statements. Consequently they cannot become false; they can only be circumvented
by leaving the class they describe. Identifying the boundary of each class is therefore
as important as proving the barrier, and we do so in every case: \S\ref{sec:family},
\S\ref{sec:sharpII}, \S\ref{sec:sharpIII} and \S\ref{sec:escape}.

\subsection{Notation}

$\Z[x]$ denotes the integer polynomials and $f(0)$ the constant term of $f$. For
$n\in\Z$, $\rad(n)$ is the set of distinct primes dividing $|n|$. We write
$\gcd(a,b)\ge 0$ for the nonnegative generator of the ideal $(a,b)$, with
$\gcd(0,n)=|n|$. $\vol$ is planar Lebesgue measure on $\C\cong\mathbb{R}^2$.

\begin{definition}[Distinct semiprime]
$N\in\N$ is a \emph{distinct semiprime} if $N=pq$ for primes $p\neq q$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Polynomial gcd witness]
For $f\in\Z[x]$ and $N\in\N$, the \emph{witness} of $f$ at $N$ is
$W_f(N):=\gcd\bigl(f(N),N\bigr)$. We say $f$ \emph{splits} $N$ if $1<W_f(N)<N$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Revealed primes]
For $f\in\Z[x]$ with $f(0)\neq 0$ set $\mathcal{R}(f):=\rad\bigl(f(0)\bigr)$. This finite
set depends only on $f$ and not on any input.
\end{definition}

\section{Barrier I: the polynomial (algebraic) barrier}

\subsection{The congruence at the heart of the barrier}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:cong}
For every $f\in\Z[x]$ and every $N\in\Z$ we have $N\mid f(N)-f(0)$, i.e.
$f(N)\equiv f(0)\pmod N$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Write $f(x)=\sum_{k\ge 0}c_kx^k$. For $k\ge 1$ we have $N\mid N^k$, so
$f(N)-f(0)=\sum_{k\ge1}c_kN^k$ is a multiple of $N$. Equivalently, $a-b$ divides
$f(a)-f(b)$ for all integers $a,b$; take $a=N$, $b=0$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Local and global forms}

\begin{theorem}[Polynomial barrier, local form]\label{thm:local}
Let $f\in\Z[x]$, $N\in\Z$, and let $p$ be a prime with $p\mid N$. Then
\[
  p\mid f(N)\iff p\mid f(0).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:cong}, $p\mid N\mid f(N)-f(0)$. If $p\mid f(N)$, subtracting gives
$p\mid f(0)$; if $p\mid f(0)$, adding gives $p\mid f(N)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Polynomial barrier, global form]\label{thm:global}
For every $f\in\Z[x]$ and every $N\in\N$,
\[
  W_f(N)=\gcd\bigl(f(N),N\bigr)=\gcd\bigl(f(0),N\bigr).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:cong} write $f(N)=f(0)+Nk$. Adding a multiple of the second argument
to the first leaves the gcd unchanged, so $\gcd(f(0)+Nk,N)=\gcd(f(0),N)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:revealed}
If $r$ is prime, $r\mid N$, $r\mid f(N)$ and $f(0)\neq 0$, then $r\in\mathcal{R}(f)$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{remark}
The evaluation $f(N)$ may require $\deg(f)\log N$ bits, but the \emph{information} it
carries about the factorization of $N$ equals that of the fixed integer $f(0)$, available
before $N$ is seen. The invariant's dependence on the input cancels identically.
\end{remark}

\subsection{The reveal budget}

\begin{theorem}[Logarithmic reveal budget]\label{thm:budget}
For $f\in\Z[x]$ with $f(0)\neq 0$ we have $\#\mathcal{R}(f)\le\log_2|f(0)|$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let $n=|f(0)|\ge 1$. The product of the distinct primes dividing $n$ divides $n$, hence
is $\le n$; each such prime is $\ge2$, so $2^{\#\mathcal{R}(f)}\le n$, and taking base-$2$
logarithms gives the claim.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Sharp failure in the cryptographic regime}

\begin{theorem}[Splitting forces a small factor]\label{thm:small}
Let $p,q$ be primes and suppose $f$ splits $N=pq$. Then $\min(p,q)\le|f(0)|$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let $w=W_f(N)$, so $1<w<N$. By Theorem~\ref{thm:global}, $w=\gcd(f(0),N)$. If $f(0)=0$
then $w=N$, contradicting $w<N$; so $f(0)\neq0$. Since $w>1$ pick a prime $r\mid w$; then
$r\mid N=pq$ and $r\mid f(0)$. Primality gives $r\in\{p,q\}$, and $r\mid f(0)\neq0$ gives
$r\le|f(0)|$. Hence $\min(p,q)\le r\le|f(0)|$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Cryptographic uselessness]\label{cor:crypto}
A polynomial invariant whose constant term has $b$ bits cannot split any semiprime both
of whose prime factors exceed $2^{b}$. Splitting a balanced $2048$-bit modulus therefore
requires a constant term of at least $1024$ bits, divisible by one of the secret primes
and written down before the modulus is seen.
\end{corollary}

\subsection{No universal family, and no bounded adaptivity}\label{sec:family}

\begin{theorem}[No universal finite family]\label{thm:family}
Let $\iota$ be a finite index set and $F:\iota\to\Z[x]$ any family. Then there is a
distinct semiprime $N=pq$ split by no $F(i)$; moreover $p,q$ may be taken larger than
$\max_i|F(i)(0)|$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Put $B=\max_{i}|F(i)(0)|$, finite by finiteness of $\iota$. Choose a prime $p>B+1$ and
then a prime $q>p$, and set $N=pq$. If some $F(i)$ split $N$, Theorem~\ref{thm:small}
would give $p=\min(p,q)\le|F(i)(0)|\le B$, contradicting $p>B$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:single}
For every $f\in\Z[x]$ there is a distinct semiprime that $f$ fails to split.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[No residue-adaptive witness]\label{thm:adaptive}
Fix $M\ge1$ and let $F:\Z/M\Z\to\Z[x]$ be arbitrary. Then there is a distinct semiprime
$N$ such that $F(N\bmod M)$ fails to split $N$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$\Z/M\Z$ is finite, so Theorem~\ref{thm:family} yields a semiprime $N$ defeating every
member of the family, in particular the member $F(N\bmod M)$ actually used.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
Adaptivity of bounded granularity buys nothing: an adversary may consult an infinite
lookup table indexed by residue classes, but because the table has finitely many distinct
entries a single semiprime defeats them all. The proof uses only finiteness of the index
set --- which is exactly why the loophole of \S\ref{sec:escape} is not covered.
\end{remark}

\subsection{Extension to rational and integer-valued invariants}

Many proposed invariants are rational: an integer $g(N)$ divided by a fixed denominator
$m$. Write $mv_N=g(N)$ and $mv_0=g(0)$.

\begin{theorem}[Scaled barrier]\label{thm:scaled}
Let $r$ be a prime with $r\mid N$ and $r\mid v_N$. Then $r\mid m$ or $r\mid v_0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
From $mv_N-mv_0=g(N)-g(0)$ and Lemma~\ref{lem:cong}, $r\mid N\mid mv_N-mv_0$. Since
$r\mid v_N$ we get $r\mid mv_N$, hence $r\mid mv_0$; primality gives $r\mid m$ or
$r\mid v_0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Scaled barrier, dichotomy form]
If in addition $r\nmid m$, then $r\mid v_N\iff r\mid v_0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$m(v_N-v_0)=g(N)-g(0)$ is divisible by $N$, hence by $r$; as $r\nmid m$ and $r$ is prime,
$r\mid v_N-v_0$, and the equivalence follows by adding and subtracting.
\end{proof}

Passing from integral to rational invariants therefore adds exactly one new source of
primes, the denominator $m$ --- itself fixed before $N$ is seen.

\begin{remark}[Scope]
Resultants, discriminants, hyperdeterminants, characteristic polynomials of matrices with
entries polynomial in $N$, and the integer outputs of a \emph{fixed} lattice construction
followed by reduction are all polynomial or rational-polynomial functions of $N$, hence
inside the scope of Theorems~\ref{thm:global} and \ref{thm:scaled}.
\end{remark}

\section{Barrier II: the symmetry (group-theoretic) barrier}

\subsection{Unique factorization for products of two primes}

\begin{lemma}[Uniqueness of the prime pair]\label{lem:pair}
Let $p,q,a,b\in\N$ with $p,a,b$ prime and $pq=ab$. Then $(p,q)=(a,b)$ or $(p,q)=(b,a)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
$p\mid ab$, so by primality $p=a$ or $p=b$. Cancelling $p$ from $pq=ab$ gives $q=b$ in
the first case and $q=a$ in the second.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The dichotomy}

\begin{definition}[Recoverable from the modulus]
A map $D:\N\times\N\to\gamma$ is \emph{recoverable from the modulus} if there is
$G:\N\to\gamma$ with $G(pq)=D(p,q)$ for all primes $p,q$. No computability or efficiency
requirement is imposed on $G$, which makes the negative results maximally strong.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Necessity]\label{thm:nec}
If $D$ is recoverable from the modulus then $D(p,q)=D(q,p)$ for all primes $p,q$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$D(p,q)=G(pq)=G(qp)=D(q,p)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Sufficiency]\label{thm:suff}
Let $\gamma$ be nonempty and let $D$ satisfy $D(p,q)=D(q,p)$ for all primes $p,q$. Then
$D$ is recoverable from the modulus.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let $P(n)$ be the property ``$n$ is a product of two primes''. If $P(n)$ holds, choose a
pair $(a,b)$ of primes with $n=ab$ and set $G(n)=D(a,b)$; otherwise let $G(n)$ be an
arbitrary element of $\gamma$. For primes $p,q$ the chosen pair for $n=pq$ is, by
Lemma~\ref{lem:pair}, either $(p,q)$ --- giving $G(pq)=D(p,q)$ --- or $(q,p)$ --- giving
$G(pq)=D(q,p)=D(p,q)$ by symmetry.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Symmetry dichotomy]\label{thm:dich}
For $\gamma$ nonempty and any $D:\N\times\N\to\gamma$, $D$ is recoverable from the
modulus if and only if $D$ is symmetric on primes.
\end{theorem}

\subsection{Antisymmetric data is destroyed}

\begin{theorem}[No antisymmetric witness]\label{thm:anti}
Let $\gamma$ be an additive group in which $x=-x$ implies $x=0$. Let $D$ satisfy
$D(q,p)=-D(p,q)$ for all $p,q$ and $D(p,q)\neq0$ for some primes $p,q$. Then $D$ is not
recoverable from the modulus.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Otherwise Theorem~\ref{thm:nec} gives $D(p,q)=D(q,p)=-D(p,q)$, so $D(p,q)=0$, a
contradiction.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
The torsion hypothesis is needed: over $\Z/2\Z$ the quantity $D(p,q)=p+q$ is
simultaneously symmetric and antisymmetric, and nothing is lost.
\end{remark}

\begin{corollary}[The prime gap]
$(p,q)\mapsto p-q\in\Z$ is not recoverable from the modulus: $D(2,3)=-1\neq1=D(3,2)$
while $2\cdot3=3\cdot2$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[No ``first factor'' extractor]\label{cor:first}
$(p,q)\mapsto p$ is not recoverable from the modulus: it would have to return both $2$
and $3$ on input $6$.
\end{corollary}

\subsection{Sharpness: the symmetric half survives}\label{sec:sharpII}

\begin{theorem}[Sharpness]\label{thm:sharpII}
The functions $(p,q)\mapsto\min(p,q)$ and $(p,q)\mapsto p+q$ are symmetric, hence by
Theorem~\ref{thm:suff} genuine functions of the modulus.
\end{theorem}

Barrier~II is thus an obstruction to \emph{well-definedness} of antisymmetric targets and
nothing more; on its own it implies no hardness. Its content is prescriptive: state the
target symmetrically, or the specification is empty before any analysis begins.

\subsection{The abstract mechanism}

\begin{theorem}[Invariance principle]\label{thm:invariance}
Let $\enc:A\to B$ and $\sigma:A\to A$ satisfy $\enc(\sigma a)=\enc(a)$ for all $a$. Then
for every $F:B\to\gamma$ and every $a\in A$, $F(\enc(\sigma a))=F(\enc(a))$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Apply $F$ to the hypothesis.
\end{proof}

Barrier~II is the case $\enc(p,q)=pq$ with $\sigma$ the transposition. Whenever a public
encoding is invariant under a group acting on the secret space, the corresponding
components of the secret are annihilated at encoding time, before any adversary acts.

\section{Barrier III: the holomorphic rigidity barrier}

Throughout, \emph{entire} means holomorphic on all of $\C$.

\subsection{Factoring out zeros}

\begin{lemma}[Difference quotients are entire]\label{lem:dq}
If $F$ is entire and $c\in\C$, then $z\mapsto\bigl(F(z)-F(c)\bigr)/(z-c)$, extended by
$F'(c)$ at $z=c$, is entire.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
The function is holomorphic on $\C\setminus\{c\}$ and bounded near $c$ with limit
$F'(c)$, so the singularity is removable by Riemann's removable singularity theorem.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[One zero factors out]\label{thm:onezero}
If $F$ is entire and $F(a)=0$, there is an entire $G$ with $F(z)=(z-a)G(z)$ for all $z$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Take $G$ the difference quotient at $c=a$; since $F(a)=0$ we get
$(z-a)G(z)=F(z)-F(a)=F(z)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Holomorphic rigidity]\label{thm:rigid}
Let $F$ be entire with $F(a)=F(b)=0$ and $a\neq b$. Then there is an entire $G$ with
\[
  F(z)=(z-a)(z-b)\,G(z)\qquad\text{for all }z\in\C.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:onezero}, $F(z)=(z-a)G_1(z)$ with $G_1$ entire. At $z=b$:
$0=F(b)=(b-a)G_1(b)$ and $b\neq a$, so $G_1(b)=0$. Applying Theorem~\ref{thm:onezero} to
$G_1$ gives $G_1(z)=(z-b)G(z)$; substitute.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Sharpened rigidity: the cofactor is a unit]\label{thm:cofactor}
Let $F$ be entire with zero set exactly $\{a,b\}$, $a\neq b$. Then the entire cofactor
$G$ of Theorem~\ref{thm:rigid} satisfies $G(z)\neq0$ for all $z\notin\{a,b\}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If $G(z_0)=0$ with $z_0\notin\{a,b\}$ then $F(z_0)=(z_0-a)(z_0-b)G(z_0)=0$, so $z_0$
lies in the zero set $\{a,b\}$ --- a contradiction.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[Circularity]
If a method constructs from $N=pq$ an entire $F_N$ whose zeros are the prime factors,
Theorem~\ref{thm:rigid} says $F_N$ is divisible by the factor polynomial
$(z-p)(z-q)=z^2-(p+q)z+N$ inside the ring of entire functions, and
Theorem~\ref{thm:cofactor} says the remaining cofactor carries no zeros and hence no
further information. Producing $F_N$ is at least as hard as producing the factorization
it is meant to reveal.
\end{remark}

\subsection{The zero set is negligible}

\begin{theorem}[Countability]\label{thm:count}
If $F$ is entire and $F(z_0)\neq0$ for some $z_0$, then $\{z:F(z)=0\}$ is countable.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$F$ is analytic on the connected set $\C$ and not identically zero, so by the identity
theorem its zero set has no accumulation point in $\C$; that is, the non-vanishing set is
codiscrete. A discrete subset of a second-countable space is countable.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Null set]\label{thm:null}
If $F$ is entire and nonzero somewhere, then $\vol\{z\in\C:F(z)=0\}=0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:count} the zero set is countable, and a countable subset of the
plane has planar Lebesgue measure zero.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Zero-set search fails]\label{thm:search}
Let $F$ be entire and nonzero somewhere, and let $S\subseteq\C$ with $\vol(S)>0$. Then
$\vol\bigl(S\setminus\{z:F(z)=0\}\bigr)=\vol(S)$, and in particular $S$ contains points
where $F$ does not vanish.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$S\cap\{F=0\}$ is a subset of a null set, hence null, so removing it does not change
$\vol(S)$. If $S\setminus\{F=0\}$ were empty, $S$ would be null, contradicting
$\vol(S)>0$.
\end{proof}

Operationally, sampling points of a region and testing for a zero succeeds with
probability exactly zero. Zeros must be located by methods requiring repeated evaluation
of $F_N$ --- which requires having constructed $F_N$, which requires the factorization.
This is the \emph{evaluation circularity}.

\subsection{The arithmetic price of a zero}

\begin{theorem}[Device size lower bound]\label{thm:devsize}
Let $f\in\Z[x]$ with $f(0)\neq0$ and let $p$ be a prime with $f(p)=0$. Then $p\mid f(0)$,
hence $p\le|f(0)|$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:local} with $N=p$: $p\mid p$ and $p\mid f(p)=0$, so $p\mid f(0)$;
and $f(0)\neq0$ gives $p\le|f(0)|$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Root budget]
Let $f\in\Z[x]$ with $f(0)\neq0$ and let $S$ be a finite set of primes each a root of
$f$. Then $\#S\le\log_2|f(0)|$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:devsize} every element of $S$ lies in $\mathcal{R}(f)$; apply
Theorem~\ref{thm:budget}.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Sharpness: the devices exist}\label{sec:sharpIII}

\begin{theorem}[Existence]\label{thm:exists}
For any two distinct primes $p,q$ there is a nonzero entire function whose zero set is
exactly $\{p,q\}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Take $F(z)=(z-p)(z-q)$: a product of complex numbers vanishes iff a factor does, and
$F(p+q+1)=(q+1)(p+1)\neq0$, so $F\not\equiv0$. More generally $F(z)=(z-p)(z-q)e^{h(z)}$
works for any entire $h$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Combined analytic barrier]\label{thm:combined3}
Let $F$ be entire with zero set exactly $\{p,q\}$ for distinct primes $p\neq q$. Then
simultaneously
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
  \item $\vol\{z:F(z)=0\}=0$, so the zero set cannot be found by search; and
  \item there is an entire $G$ with $F(z)=(z-p)(z-q)G(z)$, so the device cannot be built
        without the factorization.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$p+q+1\notin\{p,q\}$ since it exceeds both, so $F(p+q+1)\neq0$ and $F$ is nonzero
somewhere. Now (i) is Theorem~\ref{thm:null} and (ii) is Theorem~\ref{thm:rigid}.
\end{proof}

Theorem~\ref{thm:exists} prevents over-claiming: the obstruction is rigidity, not
nonexistence. The object exists and is unique up to a nonvanishing entire factor; what is
impossible is producing it from $N$ alone.

\section{The boundary: what escapes, and how}\label{sec:escape}

\subsection{Correctness of the \texorpdfstring{$p-1$}{p-1} strategy}

\begin{lemma}[Fermat, divisibility form]\label{lem:fermat}
Let $p$ be prime, $a\in\Z$ with $p\nmid a$, and $(p-1)\mid m$. Then $p\mid a^m-1$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Write $m=(p-1)t$. In $\Z/p\Z$, $a$ is invertible and $a^{p-1}=1$, so $a^m=1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[$p-1$ splits]\label{thm:pollard}
Let $p\neq q$ be primes, $N=pq$, $a\in\Z$, $m\in\N$. If $(p-1)\mid m$, $p\nmid a$ and
$q\nmid a^m-1$, then $\gcd(a^m-1,N)=p$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let $d=\gcd(a^m-1,N)$, so $d\mid pq$. By Lemma~\ref{lem:fermat}, $p\mid a^m-1$ and
$p\mid N$, hence $p\mid d$. If $q\mid d$ then $q\mid a^m-1$, excluded; so $\gcd(d,q)=1$
and $d\mid pq$ forces $d\mid p$. With $p\mid d$ this gives $d=p$.
\end{proof}

\begin{example}
$N=35=5\cdot7$, $a=2$, $m=4$: $\gcd(2^4-1,35)=\gcd(15,35)=5$.
\end{example}

\subsection{The over-smoothness failure mode}

\begin{theorem}[Too much smoothness]
Let $p\neq q$ be primes with $p\nmid a$, $q\nmid a$, and let $m$ be a multiple of both
$p-1$ and $q-1$. Then $\gcd(a^m-1,N)=N$ and no factor is found.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:fermat} both $p$ and $q$ divide $a^m-1$; being distinct primes they are
coprime, so $N=pq\mid a^m-1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{example}
$N=15=3\cdot5$, $a=2$, $m=4$: both $3-1$ and $5-1$ divide $4$, and $\gcd(15,15)=15$.
\end{example}

\subsection{A fixed exponent is a constant polynomial}

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:fixedexp}
For fixed $a\in\Z$, $m\in\N$ and all $N\in\N$, $\gcd(a^m-1,N)=W_c(N)$ where
$c(x):=a^m-1$ is a constant polynomial.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[No universal fixed exponent]\label{thm:noexp}
For every fixed $a$ and $m$ there is a distinct semiprime $N$ with
$\gcd(a^m-1,N)\in\{1,N\}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:fixedexp} the strategy is the polynomial witness of a constant
polynomial; apply Corollary~\ref{cor:single}. Concretely, take both primes larger than
$|a^m-1|$ and invoke Theorem~\ref{thm:small}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Escape requires a growing exponent]
Both of the following hold: there exist $N,a,m$ with $N$ a distinct semiprime and
$\gcd(a^m-1,N)$ a nontrivial divisor of $N$; and for every fixed $(a,m)$ there is a
distinct semiprime on which $\gcd(a^m-1,N)$ is trivial.
\end{theorem}

\begin{example}
With $a=2$, $m=12$ we have $a^m-1=4095=3^2\cdot5\cdot7\cdot13$, so the reveal set is
$\{3,5,7,13\}$; on $N=4099\cdot4111=16850989$ the witness is $1$.
\end{example}

All of the power of the $p-1$ strategy therefore comes from letting the exponent --- and
hence the bit-size of the invariant --- grow with the input. This is the one loophole
Barrier~I leaves open, and precisely the door every successful general-purpose factoring
algorithm walks through: the quadratic sieve, the number field sieve and the
elliptic-curve method are not fixed formulas evaluated at $N$ but unbounded processes
whose descriptions grow with $\log N$.

\section{How the three barriers interact}

\subsection{Bridge III \texorpdfstring{$\to$}{->} I}
Theorem~\ref{thm:devsize} shows that an integer polynomial with a prime factor among its
roots has that prime dividing its constant term: prime roots lie in $\mathcal{R}(f)$, so
the analytic strategy inherits Barrier~I's reveal budget verbatim.

\subsection{Bridge I \texorpdfstring{$\to$}{->} II}
\begin{proposition}
For every $f\in\Z[x]$ the quantity $D(p,q):=W_f(pq)$ is recoverable from the modulus
(take $G=W_f$), hence symmetric.
\end{proposition}
Barrier~I methods are therefore confined to the symmetric world of Barrier~II, and cannot
even aim at an antisymmetric target.

\subsection{Independence: II does not imply I}
\begin{theorem}\label{thm:indep}
For every $f\in\Z[x]$ it is false that $W_f(pq)=\min(p,q)$ for all distinct primes $p,q$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If it held then, since both primes are $\ge2$, we would have $1<\min(p,q)<pq$, so $f$
would split every distinct semiprime, contradicting Corollary~\ref{cor:single}.
\end{proof}

Combined with Theorem~\ref{thm:sharpII}: $\min(p,q)$ passes Barrier~II yet is blocked by
Barrier~I. The two barriers cut along genuinely different lines.

\subsection{The combined statement}
\begin{theorem}[Three barriers]
Let $p\neq q$ be primes, $N=pq$, and $f\in\Z[x]$ arbitrary. Then
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
  \item \emph{(algebraic)} $W_f(N)=\gcd(f(0),N)$, and $f$ fails to split some distinct
        semiprime;
  \item \emph{(group-theoretic)} the gap $(a,b)\mapsto a-b$ is not recoverable from the
        modulus;
  \item \emph{(analytic)} there is a nonzero entire $F$ with zero set exactly $\{p,q\}$;
        every such $F$ has $\vol\{F=0\}=0$ and factors as $F(z)=(z-p)(z-q)G(z)$ with $G$
        entire.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{Collapse evaluation.} Compute $W_f(N)$ either naively as $\gcd(f(N),N)$ or by
the barrier as $\gcd(f(0),N)$. The two always agree; the naive route costs
$\Theta(\deg f)$ multiplications on $\Theta(\deg f\cdot\log N)$-bit integers, the barrier
route a single Euclidean algorithm in $O(\log^2N)$ bit operations.

\paragraph{Adversarial modulus construction.} Given $f_1,\dots,f_k$, put
$B=\max_i|f_i(0)|$, find the least prime $p>B+1$ and least prime $q>p$, return $N=pq$. By
Theorem~\ref{thm:family} every $f_i$ fails on $N$. Cost: $O(k)$ constant-term evaluations
plus two prime searches of expected $O(\log B)$ primality tests, i.e.\ polynomial in the
description length of the family.

\paragraph{Reveal-set computation.} Factor $f(0)$ and return $\mathcal{R}(f)$: a complete
description of the invariant's lifetime power, computable before any input is seen, of
cardinality at most $\log_2|f(0)|$.

\paragraph{Symmetry audit.} Test a proposed target $D(p,q)$ against $D(q,p)$ on sample
prime pairs. A single disagreement certifies, by Theorem~\ref{thm:nec}, that no function
of $N$ implements the specification --- refuting the proposal in constant time.

\paragraph{Rigidity extraction.} Given a device $F$ and claimed zeros $p,q$, divide out
$(z-p)$ and then $(z-q)$ by difference quotients (entire by Lemma~\ref{lem:dq}) to exhibit
the cofactor $G$ with $F(z)=(z-p)(z-q)G(z)$.

\section{Applications and consequences}

\paragraph{Cryptanalysis.} Corollary~\ref{cor:crypto} gives a triage rule: a proposed
invariant is dismissed by inspecting one number, its constant term. If $|f(0)|<2^{1024}$
then $f$ cannot split any balanced $2048$-bit modulus. Corollary~\ref{cor:first} likewise
dismisses ``return the first factor'' specifications immediately.

\paragraph{Algorithm design.} The barriers are prescriptive: let the description of the
invariant grow with $\log N$; state targets symmetrically; and avoid analytic
constructions whose definition presupposes the answer.

\paragraph{Beyond factoring.} Theorem~\ref{thm:invariance} applies to any cryptographic
encoding invariant under a group action on the secret space, and Lemma~\ref{lem:cong} to
any scheme evaluating a fixed polynomial at a public modulus.

\section{Discussion}

These theorems do \emph{not} prove that factoring is hard. Barrier~I concerns
fixed-description invariants; Barrier~II is a well-definedness statement with an explicit
sharpness counterpart; Barrier~III concerns constructions, and its sharpness clause says
the devices exist. What they do prove is that three large, recurrent classes of proposal
are provably empty, for three independent structural reasons. A single ``master'' barrier
would be less informative: knowing that a proposal falls to Barrier~I but not Barrier~II
tells the designer which feature to change.

The unifying moral is one of \emph{description length}. A fixed $f$ has a fixed $f(0)$,
hence a fixed reveal budget; a specification blind to nothing but the ordering is exactly
a symmetric one; an entire function with prescribed zeros has those zeros in its
description. Escape is possible only where description length is allowed to grow --- which
is exactly where the successful algorithms live.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{conjecture}[Size--yield law for arithmetic circuits]
For every invariant computed by an arithmetic circuit of size $s$ over $\Z$ on input $N$,
any prime revealed by the gcd witness is bounded by a function of $s$ alone,
conjecturally $2^{2^{O(s)}}$, independent of the input. \emph{Falsifiable test:} exhibit a
small circuit whose gcd witness splits a semiprime both of whose primes exceed the
circuit's value at input $0$.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Adaptivity threshold]
Barrier~I survives adaptivity of bounded granularity (Theorem~\ref{thm:adaptive}) but
fails once the family is indexed by $\log N$ bits: there is a family $(f_k)$ with
$\deg f_k$ and height at most $2^k$ such that $f_{\lceil\log N\rceil}$ splits every
semiprime $N$ with $2^k$-smooth $p-1$. \emph{Falsifiable test:} find a single polynomial
family of bounded height splitting all semiprimes below a bound where both failure modes
of \S\ref{sec:escape} occur.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Antisymmetry is the only obstruction, quantitatively]
Define the antisymmetric defect of a factor-recovery scheme $D$ as the number of prime
pairs below $x$ with $D(p,q)\neq D(q,p)$. Any scheme with positive defect is not a
function of $N$; conversely every symmetric $D$ is realized by a function of $N$ whose
descriptive complexity is within $O(\log x)$ of that of the factoring oracle.
\end{conjecture}

Beyond these: extend Barrier~III to meromorphic and several-variable devices; extend
Barrier~I to invariants over number fields or $p$-adic constructions; and quantify the
adaptivity trade-off into a description-length hierarchy for factoring strategies.

\section{Conclusion}

Three theorems, three mechanisms, one moral. The polynomial barrier says
$\gcd(f(N),N)=\gcd(f(0),N)$, so a fixed invariant reveals at most $\log_2|f(0)|$ primes
ever and splitting $N=pq$ forces $\min(p,q)\le|f(0)|$. The symmetry barrier says a
quantity attached to prime pairs is a function of their product exactly when it is
symmetric, so the antisymmetric half of the factor data is destroyed at encoding time.
The holomorphic rigidity barrier says an entire function vanishing at $p$ and $q$ is
$(z-p)(z-q)$ times an entire function with nonvanishing cofactor, so constructing such a
device already encodes the factorization, while its zero set is null and invisible to
search. The three are independent and complementary, and their common boundary is
description length.

